1229
00:50:17,148 --> 00:50:18,250
- Right here.

1230
00:50:21,553 --> 00:50:23,355
- Well, I see you're
in a good mood.

1231
00:50:23,355 --> 00:50:24,222
(Joel imitates cat hissing)

1232
00:50:24,222 --> 00:50:25,923
- On the contrary.

1233
00:50:25,923 --> 00:50:26,992
I'm in a savage mood.

1234
00:50:26,992 --> 00:50:27,925
(Tom growls)

1235
00:50:27,925 --> 00:50:29,460
I can't stand incompetence.

1236
00:50:29,460 --> 00:50:32,463
You were to come here
alone on this phony rescue.

1237
00:50:32,463 --> 00:50:34,298
- [Crow] Funky rescue.

1238
00:50:37,602 --> 00:50:39,203
- Talk about incompetence.

1239
00:50:39,203 --> 00:50:41,205
In three days, you've
only managed to destroy

1240
00:50:41,205 --> 00:50:44,810
three of these interfering fools
with all your magic powers.

1241
00:50:44,810 --> 00:50:47,278
- Not all my magic
powers, Branton.

1242
00:50:47,278 --> 00:50:48,647
- [Joel] Uh, most of them.

1243
00:50:48,647 --> 00:50:50,182
- Give me my ring,

1244
00:50:50,182 --> 00:50:52,817
and I'll dispose of
these men in quick order.

1245
00:50:52,817 --> 00:50:54,552
- [Crow] Not til I
get my bicycle back.

1246
00:50:54,552 --> 00:50:56,922
- What use is the ring to you?

1247
00:50:56,922 --> 00:51:00,092
No mortal can command its magic.

1248
00:51:00,092 --> 00:51:02,860
- You'll get your ring back
when I get the princess.

1249
00:51:02,860 --> 00:51:04,162
That was our bargain.

1250
00:51:04,162 --> 00:51:05,263
- Don't you trust me?

1251
00:51:05,263 --> 00:51:06,532
- [Joel] Don't you trust me?

1252
00:51:06,532 --> 00:51:08,133
- You're insolent!

1253
00:51:08,133 --> 00:51:09,768
- [Tom] No one in
my organization

1254
00:51:09,768 --> 00:51:12,503
has ever talked to me that way.

1255
00:51:12,503 --> 00:51:14,172
- And take the ring!

1256
00:51:15,140 --> 00:51:16,508
- Go ahead.

1257
00:51:16,508 --> 00:51:18,643
- Give me my ring, now.

1258
00:51:18,643 --> 00:51:20,112
Now!

1259
00:51:20,112 --> 00:51:21,547
- [Crow] You gonna cry, crybaby?

1260
00:51:21,547 --> 00:51:24,115
Cry, you're gonna cry.

1261
00:51:24,115 --> 00:51:26,518
- But once you have it back,

1262
00:51:26,518 --> 00:51:29,220
what's to save me
from your curses?

1263
00:51:29,220 --> 00:51:31,723
- [Tom] You're wonderful.

1264
00:51:31,723 --> 00:51:35,960
- [Joel] Well, I've prepared
a small demonstration.

1265
00:51:35,960 --> 00:51:37,828
- Why would I want to hurt you?

1266
00:51:37,828 --> 00:51:38,763
- [Crow] 'Cause you're a jerk!

1267
00:51:38,763 --> 00:51:40,232
- Why wouldn't you?

1268
00:51:40,232 --> 00:51:41,600
You're only helping me now

1269
00:51:41,600 --> 00:51:43,033
because you want to
get back this ring,

1270
00:51:43,033 --> 00:51:44,302
which you so stupidly lost.

1271
00:51:44,302 --> 00:51:45,703
- [Joel] Sounds like
a Noel Coward play.

1272
00:51:45,703 --> 00:51:47,238
- I'll keep my word.

1273
00:51:47,238 --> 00:51:50,242
I'll give it back in five
days' time, when these curses

1274
00:51:50,242 --> 00:51:52,777
are behind me and I've
claimed the princess.

1275
00:51:52,777 --> 00:51:54,678
- [Tom] Please, keep
the smock as our gift.

1276
00:51:54,678 --> 00:51:56,615
- You're a tough
trader, Branton.

1277
00:51:56,615 --> 00:51:59,283
- We both know what we want,
and we're both going to get it

1278
00:51:59,283 --> 00:52:02,253
so long as you dispose
of these knights,

1279
00:52:02,253 --> 00:52:03,788
especially Sir George.

1280
00:52:03,788 --> 00:52:07,858
- [Joel] You Can Negotiate
Anything, by Basil Rathbone.

1281
00:52:07,858 --> 00:52:09,493
- Twice he's squirmed
out of your trap

1282
00:52:09,493 --> 00:52:10,695
and he could do it again.

1283
00:52:10,695 --> 00:52:11,897
- Oh, stop that.

1284
00:52:11,897 --> 00:52:13,598
You're worse than an old woman.

1285
00:52:13,598 --> 00:52:15,100
- [Crow] Yeah, he should know.

1286
00:52:15,100 --> 00:52:18,035
- If George and his knights
are not dead by week's end...

1287
00:52:18,035 --> 00:52:19,537
- They'll die long before that.

1288
00:52:19,537 --> 00:52:22,407
- [Joel] Look, you can see the
string that works his mouth.

1289
00:52:22,407 --> 00:52:25,977
- Worse than anything
I've conjured up yet.

1290
00:52:27,112 --> 00:52:30,748
One of them's coming
here now, to his death.

1291
00:52:33,318 --> 00:52:35,487
- [Tom] Stop following me!

1292
00:52:38,423 --> 00:52:39,323
- Well.

1293
00:52:39,323 --> 00:52:41,392
- [Crow] Hello, Uhura.

1294
00:52:41,392 --> 00:52:43,093
- The gallant Frenchman.

1295
00:52:43,093 --> 00:52:45,230
- [Joel] You don't
see many of those.

1296
00:52:45,230 --> 00:52:49,067
(Crow singing in French)

1297
00:52:49,067 --> 00:52:50,268
- He's seen my horse.

1298
00:52:50,268 --> 00:52:51,202
- It's all right.

1299
00:52:51,202 --> 00:52:52,670
- [Crow] And he is a Frenchman.

1300
00:52:52,670 --> 00:52:54,572
- Don't lose your head.

1301
00:52:55,940 --> 00:52:59,677
Let him come in here,
we'll be ready for him.

1302
00:53:01,747 --> 00:53:03,382
- [Tom] So, why does the
most evil man in the world

1303
00:53:03,382 --> 00:53:04,682
live in a Stuckey's?

1304
00:53:04,682 --> 00:53:06,317
- [Joel] He likes
the pecan pralines?

1305
00:53:06,317 --> 00:53:07,785
- [Crow] It's the
broasted chicken.

1306
00:53:07,785 --> 00:53:09,120
- [Tom] Nut log.

1307
00:53:10,688 --> 00:53:14,993
(girl singing Frere Jacques)

1308
00:53:14,993 --> 00:53:17,995
(All singing along)

1309
00:53:21,232 --> 00:53:24,068
(speaking French)

1310
00:53:27,405 --> 00:53:30,208
- [Tom] What would
you do, folks?

1311
00:53:30,208 --> 00:53:31,308
- Mignonette.

1312
00:53:33,878 --> 00:53:35,080
(grunting)

1313
00:53:35,080 --> 00:53:38,583
- [Joel] Sleeping in
armor is hard on a body.

1314
00:53:42,753 --> 00:53:45,223
- [Crow] Ah, just let it ring.

1315
00:53:47,825 --> 00:53:50,428
- [Tom] Huh, armor with memory.

1316
00:53:55,000 --> 00:53:58,168
- [Joel] You taste
like Charles Boyer.

1317
00:53:58,168 --> 00:53:59,870
- [Tom] So is this
French kissing?

1318
00:53:59,870 --> 00:54:01,305
(laughing)

1319
00:54:01,305 --> 00:54:04,108
(speaking French)

1320
00:54:05,343 --> 00:54:06,443
- [Crow] Yes, and
those Americans,

1321
00:54:06,443 --> 00:54:08,313
they can't appreciate
a good meal.

1322
00:54:08,313 --> 00:54:10,048
(speaking French)

1323
00:54:10,048 --> 00:54:13,285
- [Joel] Oh Tish,
you spoke French!

1324
00:54:13,285 --> 00:54:16,187
- Mademoiselle--
- uh-uh, uh-uh, no.

1325
00:54:16,187 --> 00:54:19,523
- [Tom] Come come, we've been
dating for almost two minutes.

1326
00:54:19,523 --> 00:54:21,192
(speaking French)

1327
00:54:21,192 --> 00:54:23,260
- [Crow] He's Pepe LePew!

1328
00:54:26,330 --> 00:54:27,998
- [Joel] Cat people.

1329
00:54:30,502 --> 00:54:35,440
- [Tom] Boy, he really had the
beer goggles on, didn't he?

1330
00:54:35,440 --> 00:54:37,575
- [Crow] Hey all
right everybody,
Frenchie's gettin' some!

1331
00:54:37,575 --> 00:54:39,477
(Tom cheering)

1332
00:54:39,477 --> 00:54:43,982
- [Joel] Oh man, I know
he's French, but her?

1333
00:54:43,982 --> 00:54:48,420
- [Tom] Spock!
(high-pitched droning)

1334
00:54:51,657 --> 00:54:54,725
- [Crow] Ah, Barbara
Streisand nails.

1335
00:54:58,128 --> 00:55:00,898
- [Tom] What is
your secret, man?

1336
00:55:02,000 --> 00:55:03,233
- That was close.

1337
00:55:03,233 --> 00:55:04,602
- [Joel] Hey come on,
she was just a rebound.

1338
00:55:04,602 --> 00:55:07,638
- You saved my life, mon ami.

1339
00:55:07,638 --> 00:55:10,775
We Frenchmen, we have a
weakness for pretty women.

1340
00:55:10,775 --> 00:55:12,777
- [Crow] Is that relevant?

1341
00:55:12,777 --> 00:55:15,980
- Yes, I should have known
that such a pretty creature

1342
00:55:15,980 --> 00:55:18,483
will not be at this early hour.

1343
00:55:18,483 --> 00:55:22,220
But I was robbed of all
thoughts, except one.

1344
00:55:22,220 --> 00:55:24,988
(goofy laughing)

1345
00:55:26,323 --> 00:55:29,127
- Why did you come here?
- [Tom] I am French, you know.

1346
00:55:29,127 --> 00:55:30,695
- I don't remember.

1347
00:55:31,697 --> 00:55:33,665
Wait, mon dieu.

1348
00:55:33,665 --> 00:55:34,498
- What is it?

1349
00:55:34,498 --> 00:55:35,333
- Branton.

1350
00:55:35,333 --> 00:55:36,167
- What about him?

1351
00:55:36,167 --> 00:55:37,568
- He's in that mill.

1352
00:55:37,568 --> 00:55:39,203
I'm sure of it, I saw his horse.

1353
00:55:39,203 --> 00:55:40,405
Sacre bleu.

1354
00:55:40,405 --> 00:55:43,307
- [Tom] Did I mention
that I am French?

1355
00:55:44,175 --> 00:55:45,510
- [Joel] Do we need a key?

1356
00:55:45,510 --> 00:55:48,012
- [Crow] No, just walk right in.

1357
00:55:48,012 --> 00:55:49,547
- [Tom] Hello, I am French.

1358
00:55:49,547 --> 00:55:52,283
- [Crow] Let's look at
the menu before we order.

1359
00:55:52,283 --> 00:55:53,652
- Sir Branton!

1360
00:55:53,652 --> 00:55:56,553
- [Dennis] Signoir Branton?

1361
00:55:56,553 --> 00:55:58,322
- Well, good morning gentlemen.

1362
00:55:58,322 --> 00:56:00,792
- [Tom] Two for smoking, then?

1363
00:56:02,160 --> 00:56:03,695
I am French you
know, very French.

1364
00:56:03,695 --> 00:56:04,828
- Is it good?

1365
00:56:04,828 --> 00:56:06,097
- [Joel] Sure, but is it safe?

1366
00:56:06,097 --> 00:56:08,032
- May we ask what
you're doing here?

1367
00:56:08,032 --> 00:56:10,000
- Someone has to do
the thinking for you.

1368
00:56:10,000 --> 00:56:11,702
As your commander,
I thought it wise

1369
00:56:11,702 --> 00:56:13,605
to spy out the countryside.

1370
00:56:13,605 --> 00:56:16,340
The upper floor of this mill
was the only vantage point.

1371
00:56:16,340 --> 00:56:20,845
- The last time you spied out
the land, Sir Anthony died.

1372
00:56:20,845 --> 00:56:22,547
- You can always turn
back, Sir Dennis.

1373
00:56:22,547 --> 00:56:24,615
- You have heard
my answer to that.

1374
00:56:24,615 --> 00:56:25,783
- As you will.

1375
00:56:26,952 --> 00:56:29,753
Shall we wake the
others and ride on?

1376
00:56:31,222 --> 00:56:33,792
- [Joel] Can't argue with
this guy, he's real smart.

1377
00:56:33,792 --> 00:56:34,758
- Gentlemen.

1378
00:56:34,758 --> 00:56:36,193
- [Crow] Ladies.

1379
00:56:36,193 --> 00:56:39,497
- [Tom] Age before beauty,
pearls before swine.

1380
00:56:41,165 --> 00:56:44,335
- [Crow] I know,
don't get me started.

1381
00:56:45,170 --> 00:56:46,003
- [Tom] Okay, pan right.

1382
00:56:46,003 --> 00:56:46,838
Pan right.

1383
00:56:46,838 --> 00:56:47,872
- [Joel] Keep panning.

1384
00:56:47,872 --> 00:56:49,407
- [Tom] Pan, pan, pan.

1385
00:56:49,407 --> 00:56:50,242
Pan pizza.

1386
00:56:50,242 --> 00:56:52,143
Personal pan.

1387
00:56:52,143 --> 00:56:53,845
(yelling)

1388
00:56:53,845 --> 00:56:56,747
- I almost had him, I tried.

1389
00:56:56,747 --> 00:56:59,817
I could have killed them
both, but that shield.

1390
00:56:59,817 --> 00:57:00,918
I couldn't face it.

1391
00:57:00,918 --> 00:57:04,088
- [Crow] You look like Worf.

1392
00:57:04,088 --> 00:57:05,657
- Yes, that shield.

1393
00:57:05,657 --> 00:57:07,858
- [Joel] Mark Shields, Brooke
Shields, Shields and Yarnell.

1394
00:57:07,858 --> 00:57:09,927
- Of course the boy has magic.

1395
00:57:09,927 --> 00:57:13,230
How else could he have
escaped three curses?

1396
00:57:13,230 --> 00:57:14,798
Magic

1397
00:57:14,798 --> 00:57:16,733
- [Tom] Courage!

1398
00:57:16,733 --> 00:57:20,205
- That foster mother Sybil
is working against me.

1399
00:57:20,205 --> 00:57:22,607
- His magic is strong, Lodac.

1400
00:57:22,607 --> 00:57:23,775
I know it.

1401
00:57:23,775 --> 00:57:25,743
How will you counter it?

1402
00:57:25,743 --> 00:57:27,545
Won't you need your ring?

1403
00:57:27,545 --> 00:57:30,182
- Not for Sybil's magic.
- [Joel] I can't look at you.

1404
00:57:30,182 --> 00:57:31,817
- When I get my ring...

1405
00:57:31,817 --> 00:57:33,718
- [Crow] I can do most anything!

1406
00:57:33,718 --> 00:57:36,353
- The princess will
be fed to my dragon.

1407
00:57:36,353 --> 00:57:38,155
All I have to do now is
to redouble my magic.

1408
00:57:38,155 --> 00:57:39,823
- [Tom] Is she on a spring?
- [Joel] Bobblehead.

1409
00:57:39,823 --> 00:57:41,792
- Was destroy that Frenchman!

1410
00:57:41,792 --> 00:57:43,227
You were too slow.

1411
00:57:43,227 --> 00:57:44,428
You need to be taught a lesson.

1412
00:57:44,428 --> 00:57:45,263
- No!

1413
00:57:45,263 --> 00:57:47,265
- Crawl, hag, crawl!

1414
00:57:47,265 --> 00:57:49,300
For five, six, ten hours.

1415
00:57:49,300 --> 00:57:51,870
Crawl until I need you again.

1416
00:57:51,870 --> 00:57:53,403
- [Crow] Help me, help me.

1417
00:57:53,403 --> 00:57:54,772
- [Joel] She's
finally the size two

1418
00:57:54,772 --> 00:57:56,773
she always wanted to be.

1419
00:57:58,108 --> 00:57:59,210
- Greetings, Sybil.

1420
00:57:59,210 --> 00:58:00,143
- [Tom] Ahh!

1421
00:58:00,143 --> 00:58:00,978
- Lodac.

1422
00:58:02,047 --> 00:58:03,982
- You're looking older.

1423
00:58:03,982 --> 00:58:05,283
- Thank you, Charlie Zan.

1424
00:58:05,283 --> 00:58:07,852
- What, a hundred years?

1425
00:58:07,852 --> 00:58:10,655
- I don't want to talk to you.
- But I want to talk to you.

1426
00:58:10,655 --> 00:58:11,822
- [Tom] About Amway.

1427
00:58:11,822 --> 00:58:13,525
- You've caused me
quite a lot of trouble.

1428
00:58:13,525 --> 00:58:15,158
- Lodac, if you've
touched a hair

1429
00:58:15,158 --> 00:58:17,362
on my boy's head,
I'll fight you.

1430
00:58:17,362 --> 00:58:18,563
- [Joel] Weird sexual tension.

1431
00:58:18,563 --> 00:58:20,030
- Not a chance.

1432
00:58:20,030 --> 00:58:22,333
You never had any really
talent for witchcraft.

1433
00:58:22,333 --> 00:58:24,168
- [Crow] You're a hack.

1434
00:58:24,168 --> 00:58:25,770
- Tenth-rate?

1435
00:58:25,770 --> 00:58:28,238
- You're quite helpless, Sybil.

1436
00:58:28,238 --> 00:58:29,873
In four days, the
princess Helene

1437
00:58:29,873 --> 00:58:32,243
will be fed to my dragon.

1438
00:58:32,243 --> 00:58:34,812
And your George will die
even sooner than that.

1439
00:58:34,812 --> 00:58:36,780
There's nothing you can do

1440
00:58:36,780 --> 00:58:41,318
except look in your stupid
mirror or magic pool.

1441
00:58:41,318 --> 00:58:43,888
And now I'll take care of that.

1442
00:58:45,055 --> 00:58:46,557
- Lodac, come back, come back!

1443
00:58:46,557 --> 00:58:47,725
- [Tom] Oh, now she'll
have to hang around

1444
00:58:47,725 --> 00:58:50,160
all day Tuesday waiting
for the cable guy.

1445
00:58:50,160 --> 00:58:52,630
- Mirror of magic, bring
me a vision of Lodac.

1446
00:58:52,630 --> 00:58:53,632
- [Crow] No.

1447
00:58:56,167 --> 00:58:59,970
- [Tom] Well, guess I'll just
have to read a good book then.

1448
00:58:59,970 --> 00:59:03,073
Let's see, Jurassic
Park, The Firm...

1449
00:59:04,008 --> 00:59:06,843
- [Joel] Red room, red room.

1450
00:59:06,843 --> 00:59:08,412
- Tenth-rate, am I?

1451
00:59:11,082 --> 00:59:12,950
Helpless, am I?

1452
00:59:12,950 --> 00:59:15,185
- [Tom] It's Estelle
Winwood's one-woman show.

1453
00:59:15,185 --> 00:59:16,387
- [Joel] Give 'em hell, Estelle.

1454
00:59:16,387 --> 00:59:17,588
(chuckling)

1455
00:59:17,588 --> 00:59:19,723
- [Crow] I'd go see that.

1456
00:59:19,723 --> 00:59:21,725
- So, I can't do
anything to help George.

1457
00:59:21,725 --> 00:59:23,862
- [Joel] I'm going
to get loaded.

1458
00:59:23,862 --> 00:59:25,095
- [Crow] Hey, knock it off!

1459
00:59:25,095 --> 00:59:26,863
- Lodac, you'll
be sorry for this.

1460
00:59:26,863 --> 00:59:29,500
- [Joel] Oh, there's an early
Krups machine right there.

1461
00:59:29,500 --> 00:59:33,437
- It's been a long time
since I've tried this one.

1462
00:59:33,437 --> 00:59:36,942
♪ Been a long time since
I've rocked and rolled ♪

1463
00:59:36,942 --> 00:59:37,942
- One, I'm sure

1464
00:59:37,942 --> 00:59:41,112
Where is the unicorn powder?

1465
00:59:41,112 --> 00:59:41,947
Oooh.

1466
00:59:43,013 --> 00:59:44,082
- [All] Oooh.

1467
00:59:46,483 --> 00:59:50,153
- [Crow] She should
not be left alone.

1468
00:59:50,153 --> 00:59:51,122
- [Tom] She's the type who
writes to Dear Abby, you know.

1469
00:59:51,122 --> 00:59:54,658
(instrumental theme music)

1470
00:59:56,827 --> 00:59:57,828
- [Crow] Hey keep
it down in there,

1471
00:59:57,828 --> 00:59:59,563
people work in the morning!

1472
00:59:59,563 --> 01:00:01,532
- I wonder what
Sybil's cooking up.

1473
01:00:01,532 --> 01:00:03,533
- [Joel] Trouble.
(goofy laughing)

1474
01:00:03,533 --> 01:00:05,135
She done it again.

1475
01:00:05,135 --> 01:00:07,405
(laughing)

1476
01:00:10,073 --> 01:00:13,443
(humming sitcom music)

1477
01:00:13,443 --> 01:00:15,245
- [Tom] Lucy, have you
been scheming again?

1478
01:00:15,245 --> 01:00:17,582
- What have you done, Sybil?

1479
01:00:18,950 --> 01:00:20,385
- I've finished poor George,
that's what I've done.

1480
01:00:20,385 --> 01:00:24,088
I've taken his magic
away from him completely.

1481
01:00:25,323 --> 01:00:27,558
- George has no more magic,

1482
01:00:27,558 --> 01:00:30,027
(Tom makes sad trombone notes)

1483
01:00:30,027 --> 01:00:32,330
- [Joel] We'll be back
with two separate views

1484
01:00:32,330 --> 01:00:34,332
of love, American-style.

1485
01:00:40,572 --> 01:00:44,075
- [Crow] Butch and Sundance,
the really early years.

1486
01:00:44,075 --> 01:00:46,877
- James, Dennis,
where are ya now?

1487
01:00:49,280 --> 01:00:51,348
- There are the tracks
of their horses.

1488
01:00:51,348 --> 01:00:52,617
- [Tom] Or the
tracks of my tears.

1489
01:00:52,617 --> 01:00:53,885
- I can't believe
they're ahead of us.

1490
01:00:53,885 --> 01:00:55,252
- [Crow] I can't
believe it's not butter.

1491
01:00:55,252 --> 01:00:57,055
- It's gettin'
hotter by the moment.

1492
01:00:57,055 --> 01:01:00,123
- [Joel] Why don't you take
off six or seven layers?

1493
01:01:00,123 --> 01:01:01,558
- What could I do?

1494
01:01:01,558 --> 01:01:03,762
They were up before daylight,
and belligerent at that.

1495
01:01:03,762 --> 01:01:05,963
Sir Dennis said that
he couldn't trust me

1496
01:01:05,963 --> 01:01:08,098
to spy out the land any longer.

1497
01:01:08,098 --> 01:01:09,633
- You could have wakened us.

1498
01:01:09,633 --> 01:01:10,902
- [Tom] Woken.

1499
01:01:10,902 --> 01:01:11,935
- They swore they'd be
back before daylight.

1500
01:01:11,935 --> 01:01:13,670
- Dennis, James!

1501
01:01:13,670 --> 01:01:15,105
- [Tom] Stella!

1502
01:01:15,105 --> 01:01:17,842
- They'll be burnt to
a crisp by this time.

1503
01:01:17,842 --> 01:01:22,347
- [Crow] By this time, my
lungs were burnt to a crisp.

1504
01:01:23,480 --> 01:01:26,017
- [Joel] Dreezil
drozil drozil drome,

1505
01:01:26,017 --> 01:01:28,585
time for these two to come home.

1506
01:01:28,585 --> 01:01:30,555
- [Tom] Help, Mr. Wizard.

1507
01:01:30,555 --> 01:01:32,757
- [Joel] Hey, it's Peter
Garrett from Midnight Oii

1508
01:01:32,757 --> 01:01:33,992
and his double.

1509
01:01:37,162 --> 01:01:38,628
- [All] Ugh.

1510
01:01:38,628 --> 01:01:42,667
- [Crow] Looks like their
jelly donuts blew up.

1511
01:01:42,667 --> 01:01:44,102
- Get back!

1512
01:01:44,102 --> 01:01:46,537
- [Tom] To the five and
dime, Jimmy Dean, Jimmy Dean!

1513
01:01:46,537 --> 01:01:49,473
- [Joel] The hills have eyes.

1514
01:01:49,473 --> 01:01:50,273
Kind of.

1515
01:01:57,047 --> 01:01:58,415
- Dennis!
- James!

1516
01:01:59,517 --> 01:02:00,718
- [Tom] Elaine!

1517
01:02:06,157 --> 01:02:07,858
- Will you be
looking at that man?

1518
01:02:07,858 --> 01:02:09,060
- [Tom] I'm Irish, you know.

1519
01:02:09,060 --> 01:02:10,528
- He's not even hot
and we're burning, why?

1520
01:02:10,528 --> 01:02:11,462
- [Tom] Aye, I'm Irish.

1521
01:02:11,462 --> 01:02:12,830
- Yes, why, Branton?

1522
01:02:12,830 --> 01:02:15,533
- That, gentlemen, is my secret.

1523
01:02:15,533 --> 01:02:20,538
- [Crow] It's strong enough for
a man, but made for a woman.

1524
01:02:21,705 --> 01:02:22,507
- [Tom] But wait,
what's your secret?

1525
01:02:22,507 --> 01:02:23,740
Come on, tell me, wait.

1526
01:02:23,740 --> 01:02:27,245
- [Crow] I can't hear
you, I'm not listening!

1527
01:02:29,147 --> 01:02:31,182
- [Tom] Come on, you gotta
tell us, what's your secret?

1528
01:02:31,182 --> 01:02:32,283
Come on, hey!

1529
01:02:32,283 --> 01:02:33,750
- [Crow] I can't hear you!

1530
01:02:33,750 --> 01:02:36,753
(humming gibberish)

1531
01:02:38,288 --> 01:02:42,427
(all imitating
train approaching)

1532
01:02:43,693 --> 01:02:47,132
- [Joel] We're not
going in there, buddy.

1533
01:02:52,337 --> 01:02:53,537
- The horses won't
go in the cave.

1534
01:02:53,537 --> 01:02:55,540
- [Joel] Oh, I'm
not goin' in there,

1535
01:02:55,540 --> 01:02:58,508
it's not part of
the union contract.

1536
01:03:02,180 --> 01:03:03,882
- [Tom] Just park it here.

1537
01:03:03,882 --> 01:03:05,850
Lock it up, there we go.

1538
01:03:09,087 --> 01:03:10,053
- [All] Surprise!

1539
01:03:10,053 --> 01:03:11,288
♪ Happy birthday to you ♪

1540
01:03:11,288 --> 01:03:12,223
- [Tom] Oh.

1541
01:03:13,825 --> 01:03:15,527
- [George] Branton.

1542
01:03:15,527 --> 01:03:16,993
- [Joel] Echo.

1543
01:03:16,993 --> 01:03:18,897
Guys, this isn't funny.

1544
01:03:24,235 --> 01:03:25,435
- Where the devil is he?

1545
01:03:25,435 --> 01:03:26,270
- [Tom] Di-vil?

1546
01:03:26,270 --> 01:03:27,205
- [Joel] He's Irish.

1547
01:03:27,205 --> 01:03:28,438
- He's in here somewhere.

1548
01:03:28,438 --> 01:03:29,540
- [Branton] You mean
I was in here, George.

1549
01:03:29,540 --> 01:03:30,708
- Sir Branton!

1550
01:03:31,942 --> 01:03:33,978
- I'm sorry I have
to leave you know.

1551
01:03:33,978 --> 01:03:37,147
I've got a longstanding
appointment with
the princess Helene

1552
01:03:37,147 --> 01:03:39,283
and also with my partner Lodac.

1553
01:03:39,283 --> 01:03:40,183
- Partner!?

1554
01:03:41,385 --> 01:03:43,820
- Yes, his castle is
just over the hill.

1555
01:03:43,820 --> 01:03:46,323
Any last words for
either of them?

1556
01:03:46,323 --> 01:03:47,425
- [All] Help!

1557
01:03:48,558 --> 01:03:51,628
- I already have
escaped you, George.

1558
01:03:52,697 --> 01:03:53,630
- [Joel] Ladies and gentlemen,

1559
01:03:53,630 --> 01:03:56,267
the magic of David Copperfield.

1560
01:03:56,267 --> 01:03:59,370
♪ It's a brick house ♪

1561
01:04:00,503 --> 01:04:02,473
- [Crow] Okay,
where's Alan Funt?

1562
01:04:02,473 --> 01:04:05,108
- [Joel] We thought
it would be funny...

1563
01:04:05,108 --> 01:04:06,710
- We break out.

1564
01:04:06,710 --> 01:04:07,912
With Ascalon's help.

1565
01:04:07,912 --> 01:04:09,047
- Ascalon?

1566
01:04:09,047 --> 01:04:10,213
- [Crow] You
Ascalon, I'm bitter.

1567
01:04:10,213 --> 01:04:11,815
♪ Ascalon ♪

1568
01:04:11,815 --> 01:04:14,485
- Doors, walls, gates, anything.

1569
01:04:17,922 --> 01:04:18,855
(sword clanging)

1570
01:04:18,855 --> 01:04:20,057
(nervous laughing_

1571
01:04:20,057 --> 01:04:21,025
- [Tom] You see, the
batteries are kinda low.

1572
01:04:21,025 --> 01:04:22,125
It's lithium.

1573
01:04:23,727 --> 01:04:24,928
- Like your horse.

1574
01:04:24,928 --> 01:04:28,765
Perhaps Lodac's magic
is a bit stronger.

1575
01:04:28,765 --> 01:04:29,967
(sword clanging)

1576
01:04:29,967 --> 01:04:31,202
- [Joel] Please
wear safety goggles

1577
01:04:31,202 --> 01:04:32,437
If you're going
to do that, guys.

1578
01:04:32,437 --> 01:04:34,072
- You'll only be
dulling the blade.

1579
01:04:34,072 --> 01:04:35,505
- [Tom] The movie's
duller than that blade.

1580
01:04:35,505 --> 01:04:39,177
- There must be some
other way out, Patrick.

1581
01:04:40,543 --> 01:04:42,178
- [Crow] Well, I can
only conclude one thing.

1582
01:04:42,178 --> 01:04:43,013
We're gonna die!

1583
01:04:43,013 --> 01:04:45,415
(screaming)

1584
01:04:45,415 --> 01:04:47,117
- [Joel] You know,
I was just thinking,

1585
01:04:47,117 --> 01:04:48,485
a little wallpaper,
some carpeting,

1586
01:04:48,485 --> 01:04:52,323
this place could be
fixed up real nice.

1587
01:04:52,323 --> 01:04:56,327
- [Tom] I dub you Sir
Moron, come on dummy.

1588
01:04:56,327 --> 01:04:58,195
- [Joel] How come all
movies end in a cave?

1589
01:04:58,195 --> 01:04:59,530
- [Tom] To Kill a
Mockingbird didn't.

1590
01:04:59,530 --> 01:05:00,598
- [Joel] Well you're
right about that.

1591
01:05:00,598 --> 01:05:01,632
- [Crow] At Long
Last Love didn't

1592
01:05:01,632 --> 01:05:02,833
- [Joel] That's true.

1593
01:05:02,833 --> 01:05:03,935
- [Tom] To Have and
Have Not Anna Christie?

1594
01:05:03,935 --> 01:05:05,268
- [Joel] Three.

1595
01:05:05,268 --> 01:05:06,203
You're right, just
about all of 'em,

1596
01:05:06,203 --> 01:05:07,738
none of 'em ended in a cave.

1597
01:05:07,738 --> 01:05:12,343
- [Tom] What the, this place
is filled with breadsticks.

1598
01:05:13,410 --> 01:05:16,247
Hey, are we in
someone's epiglottis?

1599
01:05:18,515 --> 01:05:23,187
- [Crow] Oh, just a cheap
Bert I. Gordon special effect.

1600
01:05:24,888 --> 01:05:28,192
Nope, nothing happening
with the plot over here.

1601
01:05:28,192 --> 01:05:30,762
- [Tom] Nothing
over here either.

1602
01:05:30,762 --> 01:05:31,595
All clear.

1603
01:05:36,300 --> 01:05:38,402
- [Joel] It's Ho Chi Min.

1604
01:05:41,005 --> 01:05:41,838
- Patrick!

1605
01:05:43,073 --> 01:05:45,575
- [Crow] It's the New Monkees!

1606
01:05:47,345 --> 01:05:49,913
- [All] Guilty, guilty, guilty!

1607
01:05:53,450 --> 01:05:54,918
- [Joel] You know
what, I just realized

1608
01:05:54,918 --> 01:05:59,257
we're in The Ghost and Mr.
Chicken, let's get outta here!

1609
01:05:59,857 --> 01:06:00,792
(screaming)

1610
01:06:00,792 --> 01:06:02,092
- [Tom] Oh they're not dead,

1611
01:06:02,092 --> 01:06:04,562
they're just
metaphysically challenged.

1612
01:06:09,700 --> 01:06:10,868
- [Crow] The faces
of those he's wronged

1613
01:06:10,868 --> 01:06:12,803
are floating up at him!

1614
01:06:14,038 --> 01:06:15,707
- [Tom] Jimmy Smits.

1615
01:06:20,543 --> 01:06:23,280
[Joel] Hall's
vapor action, yeah.

1616
01:06:24,382 --> 01:06:26,717
(screaming)

1617
01:06:29,253 --> 01:06:30,722
- [Tom] So?

1618
01:06:30,722 --> 01:06:34,458
- [Joel] I am the god of
hellfire, and I bring you...

1619
01:06:35,793 --> 01:06:37,495
- [Tom] Yeah, he's battling
his own demons here.

1620
01:06:37,495 --> 01:06:40,932
- [Joel] Oh boy, this is
becoming the Green Ghost game.

1621
01:06:40,932 --> 01:06:42,700
- [Tom] Neat.

1622
01:06:42,700 --> 01:06:44,668
- [Crow] There's something
you don't see every day.

1623
01:06:44,668 --> 01:06:46,137
- [Tom] Pretty much.

1624
01:06:46,137 --> 01:06:49,373
Guy writhing in hell, soul
torn out from underneath him.

1625
01:06:49,373 --> 01:06:52,543
- [Patrick] Through the
walls, through the walls!

1626
01:06:52,543 --> 01:06:54,312
- [Tom] So I'll just leave then.

1627
01:06:54,312 --> 01:06:55,145
I guess.

1628
01:06:58,682 --> 01:07:02,285
- [Crow] Oh great, now
we're stuck in here.

1629
01:07:03,653 --> 01:07:05,288
[Tom] That was an interesting
scene, but one question.

1630
01:07:05,288 --> 01:07:06,757
What the heck just
happened here!?

1631
01:07:06,757 --> 01:07:08,192
- [Joel] Trippy.

1632
01:07:08,192 --> 01:07:11,695
- [Crow] I'll be here
if you need me, George.

1633
01:07:11,695 --> 01:07:13,597
- [Tom] He's dead, Jim.

1634
01:07:15,598 --> 01:07:17,067
- [Joel] See, you get to
pick out your own steak.

1635
01:07:17,067 --> 01:07:19,537
Watch this, I'll have that one.

1636
01:07:19,537 --> 01:07:21,105
- [Crow] Hmm, neat
stuff, but is it art?

1637
01:07:21,105 --> 01:07:23,140
- He's escaped again!

1638
01:07:23,140 --> 01:07:24,075
- Sir Patrick too.

1639
01:07:24,075 --> 01:07:25,843
- [Joel] And Toto too.

1640
01:07:25,843 --> 01:07:29,880
- Patrick is finished but Sir
George is riding this way.

1641
01:07:29,880 --> 01:07:33,117
- There was no way out
of that cave, Lodac.

1642
01:07:33,117 --> 01:07:34,050
Was it magic?

1643
01:07:34,050 --> 01:07:35,285
- [Tom] Or was it Memorex?

1644
01:07:35,285 --> 01:07:36,120
You decide.

1645
01:07:36,120 --> 01:07:37,053
- Not magic.

1646
01:07:37,053 --> 01:07:38,388
- Then how did George escape?

1647
01:07:38,388 --> 01:07:39,757
- I think, yes...

1648
01:07:41,258 --> 01:07:43,060
Something stronger than magic.

1649
01:07:43,060 --> 01:07:44,328
- [Joel] Double secret magic.

1650
01:07:44,328 --> 01:07:45,763
- [Lodac] The power
of Patrick's faith.

1651
01:07:45,763 --> 01:07:50,768
(mechanical whirring)
(doors closing)

1652
01:07:55,873 --> 01:07:57,475
- Fellas, I've got
something to say

1653
01:07:57,475 --> 01:07:58,842
and there's only
one way to do it,

1654
01:07:58,842 --> 01:08:00,377
so gimme center stage!

1655
01:08:00,377 --> 01:08:03,113
- Hey, be my guest,
it's all yours.

1656
01:08:03,113 --> 01:08:07,317
♪ As you guys I'm
sure remember ♪

1657
01:08:07,317 --> 01:08:12,322
♪ think it was maybe
last December ♪

1658
01:08:13,523 --> 01:08:16,993
♪ I fell, pell-mell,
for Kim Cattrall ♪

1659
01:08:17,995 --> 01:08:19,363
- Yeah, we remember.

1660
01:08:19,363 --> 01:08:21,298
Please don't remind us.

1661
01:08:22,667 --> 01:08:25,302
♪ But now I'm over that ♪
- Oh that's good.

1662
01:08:25,302 --> 01:08:29,607
♪ We had a little spat ♪
- In your dreams, buddy.

1663
01:08:29,607 --> 01:08:32,577
♪ I'm older, wiser and I know ♪

1664
01:08:32,577 --> 01:08:37,213
♪ that my true love
is really named ♪

1665
01:08:37,213 --> 01:08:39,750
♪ Estelle ♪

1666
01:08:39,750 --> 01:08:40,783
- [Tom] Winwood?

1667
01:08:40,783 --> 01:08:42,820
- [Crow] She's swell!

1668
01:08:42,820 --> 01:08:44,622
♪ She's cute ♪

1669
01:08:44,622 --> 01:08:46,657
♪ She's rooty-toot-toot ♪

1670
01:08:46,657 --> 01:08:49,393
♪ I bet she smells
like Juicy Fruit ♪

1671
01:08:49,393 --> 01:08:52,563
♪ She can really play a witch ♪

1672
01:08:52,563 --> 01:08:55,867
♪ She was even on Bewitched ♪

1673
01:08:55,867 --> 01:08:59,637
♪ And I'm bewildered
and bothered ♪

1674
01:08:59,637 --> 01:09:01,505
- Oh but Crow, hold
on a second, hold on.

1675
01:09:01,505 --> 01:09:02,507
- Thank you.

1676
01:09:04,542 --> 01:09:07,912
♪ Crow, you are my buddy ♪

1677
01:09:07,912 --> 01:09:11,682
♪ And maybe I'm a fuddy duddy ♪

1678
01:09:11,682 --> 01:09:13,317
♪ But step back please ♪

1679
01:09:13,317 --> 01:09:18,055
♪ And think about some stuff ♪
- like what?

1680
01:09:19,022 --> 01:09:22,593
♪ I'm sure that she seems nice ♪

1681
01:09:22,593 --> 01:09:26,430
♪ But I bet she's more
than twice your age ♪

1682
01:09:26,430 --> 01:09:29,933
♪ And with your love
it might not matter ♪

1683
01:09:29,933 --> 01:09:33,103
♪ But can she
control her bladder ♪

1684
01:09:33,103 --> 01:09:35,638
- Shut up!
- Tom, come on.

1685
01:09:35,638 --> 01:09:37,107
- Okay, okay, but Joel,

1686
01:09:37,107 --> 01:09:38,908
there's a whole other
set of issues here.

1687
01:09:38,908 --> 01:09:41,045
If I may, Crow, as a friend?

1688
01:09:41,045 --> 01:09:42,580
As a trusted adviser?

1689
01:09:42,580 --> 01:09:43,680
- Well...

1690
01:09:43,680 --> 01:09:45,315
- Okay.

1691
01:09:45,315 --> 01:09:46,750
Here's a quick list of people

1692
01:09:46,750 --> 01:09:49,920
much better looking than Estelle

1693
01:09:49,920 --> 01:09:51,588
- You're so shallow.

1694
01:09:51,588 --> 01:09:53,723
♪ Fred Biletnikoff,
Brandon Tartikoff,
Sid and Marty Krofft ♪

1695
01:09:53,723 --> 01:09:54,958
- Very funny.

1696
01:09:54,958 --> 01:09:56,260
♪ Fred Gwynne and
Anthony Quinn ♪

1697
01:09:56,260 --> 01:09:58,062
♪ And Rin Tin Tin
and Pearl Bailey ♪

1698
01:09:58,062 --> 01:09:59,830
♪ And Moms Mabley
and Mayor Daley ♪

1699
01:09:59,830 --> 01:10:02,498
♪ And Hank and Phoebe Snow ♪
- I'm not listening.

1700
01:10:02,498 --> 01:10:04,702
♪ Ethel Merman and Pee Wee
Herman and Strom Thurmond ♪

1701
01:10:04,702 --> 01:10:08,372
♪ and Vince Lombardi and
George and Jesus Jones. ♪

1702
01:10:08,372 --> 01:10:11,042
- I don't care, I don't care!
♪ Edith Head and Mister Ed ♪

1703
01:10:11,042 --> 01:10:12,810
♪ and Nostradamus
and Danny Thomas ♪

1704
01:10:12,810 --> 01:10:14,778
♪ and Leona and
Sherman Hemsley ♪

1705
01:10:14,778 --> 01:10:15,980
- Joel, make him stop!

1706
01:10:15,980 --> 01:10:18,715
- Tom, you're not
helping things at all.

1707
01:10:18,715 --> 01:10:19,983
Now, can you go and please

1708
01:10:19,983 --> 01:10:21,952
clip those coupons
like you promised?

1709
01:10:21,952 --> 01:10:23,487
- Uhhh, okay.

1710
01:10:23,487 --> 01:10:25,388
♪ Willy DeVille and
Agnes de Mille ♪

1711
01:10:25,388 --> 01:10:29,158
♪ and Cruella DeVille
and Roscoe Tanner and ♪

1712
01:10:29,158 --> 01:10:30,193
- Crow, listen.

1713
01:10:30,193 --> 01:10:31,795
Don't worry about it, friend.

1714
